        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"This study reports the MET4-IO trial (NCT03686202), explicitly describing it as an 'early phase clinical trial' (Phase I/II context) involving a 30-species microbial consortium combined with immune checkpoint inhibitors in advanced solid tumors. The authors list multiple other trial registry numbers in the references (e.g., NCT04988841, NCT04951583, NCT05251389, NCT04758507, NCT04521075, NCT04116775, NCT04208958, NCT03817125) and state in the Discussion that 'There are a total of three clinical trials registered for evaluating microbial consortia as an ICI co-therapy,' identifying their study as the first report. The text mentions a planned 'randomized, placebo-controlled phase II trial' endorsed by the Canadian Cancer Trial Group but does not identify any completed or ongoing Phase III trials in this specific indication. The paper provides specific NCT identifiers and phase characterizations (Phase I, Phase II, early phase) that serve as starting points for registry verification.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The study details safety outcomes (no MET4-related grade 3 AEs), ecological engraftment results (increases in Enterococcus and Bifidobacterium in a subset of recipients), and metabolomic changes (decreases in primary bile acids associated with engraftment). It compares microbial consortia to FMT and single-strain probiotics regarding scalability and ecological complexity. These findings support the biological plausibility and safety of microbiome-ICI combinations but do not address the registry phase status directly.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The supplied material does not fulfill the success criterion requiring documentation of a registry search strategy, date, and filters to confirm the absence or presence of Phase III trials. While the authors list several NCT identifiers and characterize the current landscape as predominantly early-phase